{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1787222697,"nonce":"6zV/dlFkLuA2G38JCWtAAUJlQJ4=","message":"Done.\n\n**The race.** A Case whose transaction commits after the tick has read its window was counted by no\nwindow at all: `started_at` is stamped before the write (`alerts/domain/lifecycle.go`, `StartedAt:\np.At.RecordedAt()`) while the cursor never went back.\n\n**Fix.** A bounded lookback rather than a lag: every tick re-reads `[T − L, T + W)` with\n`DigestLookback = 2m`, and the per-`(policy, Case)` mark table makes the re-read idempotent — a Case\nalready reported folds to zero instead of being reported twice. This is why the fix is a re-scan and\nnot the \"named lag\" the body's conditional clause asked for; comment #4 ruled that direction, and a lag\nwould have delayed every digest to pay for a straggler.\n\n`digest_tick_test.go` drives the exact race: a Case whose `started_at` precedes the previous tick's\nread but whose row appears afterwards is counted by the next digest, exactly once.\n\n**Done-when 4 also satisfied, and it was the last thing outstanding.** SPEC §G.3 now carries the\n`notify.digest.reconcile` row and states the added latency explicitly: **none** — the lookback widens\nthe *read* to `[T − 2min, T + W)` and the only latency is the 60 s tick. The alternative (delaying the\ndigest by `L`) is recorded there with the reason it was refused.\n\nGates: full `-race` suite, contract and integration suites green.","files":null}]}